For a classical system, the state space is a set. An abstract die has $\mathcal S_{\mathrm{die}}=\{1,2,3,4,5,6\}.$ A real die has continuously many orientations, but the idealized die used here has only these six labels. A particle on a line has the continuously infinite set $\mathcal S_{\mathrm{particle}}
  =
  \{(q,p)\mid q,p\in\mathbb R\}.$ Finite or infinite, each classical state space is a collection of mutually distinct possibilities.

